{
  "title": "Rayleigh surface wave dispersion of a periodic half-space cell (analytic, eigenfrequency, Bloch)",
  "description": "Rayleigh surface wave of an isotropic half space as a Bloch-periodic cell of solid_mechanics (2D, plane strain) on a quadratic triangle mesh: one period L = 1 um along x (periodic_x, bloch_kx = k_fraction 2 pi / L swept over 0.2-0.5, Gamma to the zone edge), 12 um deep with a Fixed bottom (the wave decays as exp(-0.39 k depth) for its shear part, so the bottom sits > 6 decay lengths down at the smallest k), free top surface, E 70 GPa, nu 0.25 (a Poisson solid), rho 2200 kg/m^3; an eigenfrequency study per k lists the 2 lowest bands (resonance_table 'bands'). The lowest band is the Rayleigh wave f_1 = c_R k / (2 pi) (the slab's other modes lie above c_T k, the folded branch at |k - 2 pi / L| above it too; at the zone edge the folded branch meets it: the pair of standing waves).\nAnalytic: for nu = 0.25 the Rayleigh equation (2 - xi^2)^2 = 4 sqrt(1 - xi^2) sqrt(1 - xi^2 c_T^2 / c_L^2), xi = c / c_T, has the closed-form root xi^2 = 2 - 2 / sqrt(3): c_R = 0.91940 c_T, plane-strain c_T = sqrt(mu / rho), c_L = sqrt(3) c_T.\nDerived values tab: f_1 (lowest band) against c_R k / (2 pi) and v_R = 2 pi f_1 / k against c_R per k (P2 with h = L / 4: ~1e-4); Sweep tab: f_1 over k_fraction with the analytic line f = c_R k_fraction / L. Snapshot slider: the complex Bloch modes as their real part (phase 0) and u_abs = |u| (decaying into the depth).",
  "version": 2,
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "E_s",
      "expression": 70000000000.0,
      "description": "Young's modulus [Pa]"
    },
    {
      "name": "nu_s",
